From: Robin <robin@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] GStreamer: do not wait for the main thread on a stream-collection message

handleStreamCollectionMessage() runs on a streaming thread that holds its pad's stream lock and used
callOnMainThreadAndWait() to update the tracks. When the main thread was at the same time in
gst_element_set_state() on that pipeline (managePlayerSuspend(): a muted video scrolled out of the
viewport), the state change waited for the stream lock and the streaming thread waited for the main
thread. The page froze with an idle web process (LG CX, a Reddit feed of muted autoplay videos, about 30 s
of scrolling; stacks: main thread in managePlayerSuspend -> gst_element_set_state, a multiqueue thread
in handleStreamCollectionMessage -> callOnMainThreadAndWait). The track update now runs on the main
thread without the streaming thread waiting for it.
---
--- a/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/gstreamer/MediaPlayerPrivateGStreamer.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/platform/graphics/gstreamer/MediaPlayerPrivateGStreamer.cpp
@@ -1969,9 +1969,11 @@
             player->updateTracks(owner);
     };
 
-    GST_DEBUG_OBJECT(pipeline(), "Updating tracks");
-    callOnMainThreadAndWait(WTF::move(callback));
-    GST_DEBUG_OBJECT(pipeline(), "Updating tracks DONE");
+    // Not callOnMainThreadAndWait: this runs on a streaming thread that holds its pad's stream lock, and the main thread
+    // may be in gst_element_set_state() on the same pipeline (managePlayerSuspend() for a muted video that left the
+    // viewport), which waits for that lock. Waiting here deadlocked the page (a feed of muted autoplay videos, LG TV).
+    GST_DEBUG_OBJECT(pipeline(), "Updating tracks (on the main thread, not waited for)");
+    callOnMainThread(WTF::move(callback));
 }
 
 bool MediaPlayerPrivateGStreamer::handleNeedContextMessage(GstMessage* message)
